// Linear algebra for sysl: vectors, matrices, and elimination over any element type that behaves
// like a number.
//
// One body per operation and not one per element type. `solve`, `det`, `rank` and `inverse` are
// written once against a bound and run at `real`, at `f32` and over `Complex[F]` unchanged -- which
// is the claim the test suite is built to make, since a routine that had quietly specialised to the
// reals would pass the first section and fail the complex one.
//
// This is the block a consumer writes to reach it:
//
//     dependencies {
//       linalg { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/linalg", version = "0.1.0" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate names the package, `linalg`; the module a program imports is `sh.sysl.linalg`. It
// declares no dependencies of its own -- everything it needs is the standard library's `sysl.buf`,
// `sysl.math` and `sysl.math.complex`.

package {
  name    = "linalg"
  version = "0.1.0"

  // **The oldest compiler this builds with, and the reason is how a matrix renders.** `Vector` and
  // `Matrix` lay their cells out in columns, which means knowing how wide each rendering is before
  // any of it is written -- and they measure into a `Counting` sink rather than gathering the text
  // and throwing it away. `Counting` became public in `a20ae810` and the two renderers moved onto it
  // in `6d4b3b3f`, both after 0.0.79, so an older compiler has nothing to measure with.
  //
  // Card `0284` is the one that put it there; before it, laying out a matrix built every cell's text
  // twice.
  sysl = "0.0.80"
}

// **`heap = true`, and it is the storage rather than the algebra that asks for it.** A matrix's cells
// are one `&Buf[T]` and a vector's are another, sized by the dimensions the caller passes at run
// time -- so nothing here can be given caller-supplied storage, because nothing here knows how much
// to ask for until it is called. The elimination routines then build a working copy, since reducing
// a matrix in place would consume the caller's.
//
// **What is NOT here is `os` or `posix`.** Nothing in this package opens, reads or writes anything:
// a rendering goes into a `Writer` the caller passes, and where it ends up is the program's business.
requires {
  heap = true
}
